## 2. Spanner TrueTime vs. Hybrid Logical Clocks (HLC)

To achieve strict serializability (Linearizability), transactions must be totally ordered in a way that respects real-time causality: if transaction $B$ is initiated after transaction $A$ completes globally, $B$ must receive a higher timestamp than $A$.

### 2.1 Deep-Dive Comparison

| Metric / Dimension | Google Spanner TrueTime | Hybrid Logical Clocks (HLC) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Clock Hardware** | Requires atomic clocks + GPS receivers per datacenter. | Standard NTP / PTP infrastructure (software-managed). |
| **Uncertainty Bound ($\epsilon$)** | Bounded interval $[t.earliest, t.latest]$, where $\epsilon = (latest - earliest)/2$. Typically $100\mu\text{s} - 250\mu\text{s}$ in hyper-scale clouds. | Unbounded physical drift; bound is maintained via logical counter increments. |
| **Commit Latency Penalty** | **Commit-Wait:** Must wait out $2\epsilon$ ($\sim 500\mu\text{s}$) to guarantee the timestamp is safely in the past across the universe. | **Zero Commit-Wait:** Timestamps are assigned instantly based on local physical/logical state. |
| **Strict Serializability** | **Native:** Guaranteed by the commit-wait mechanism and monotonic interval intersections. | **Conditional:** Provides *Causal+* consistency natively; requires an external global sequencer or consensus barrier to guarantee strict serializability. |
| **Failure Modes** | GPS/Atomic clock loss causes $\epsilon$ to inflate (latency spikes, safe fallback). | NTP skew or leaps cause logical counter explosions, risking causal violations if bounds are breached. |
| **Sub-ms Feasibility** | **Challenging:** $500\mu\text{s}$ commit-wait consumes half of a $1\text{ms}$ budget, leaving little room for network RTT. | **High:** Eliminates commit-wait overhead, but shifts coordination overhead to consensus rounds. |

### 2.2 Mathematical Formulations

#### Spanner TrueTime Commit-Wait
Given a commit request at local time $t = \text{TT.now()}$, the transaction is assigned timestamp $s = t.\text{latest}$. The coordinator must delay releasing the transaction results to the client until:
$$\text{TT.now().earliest} \ge s$$
This guarantees that any subsequent transaction initiated anywhere in the world will read a physical time strictly greater than $s$.

#### Hybrid Logical Clock (HLC) Update Rule
An HLC tuple is defined as $hlc = (l, c)$, where $l$ is physical time (NTP) and $c$ is a logical counter. When an event or message arrives with $hlc_{msg} = (l_{msg}, c_{msg})$:
$$\begin{aligned}
l' &= \max(l_{physical}, l, l_{msg}) \\
c' &= \begin{cases} 
c + 1 & \text{if } l' = l \text{ and } l' = l_{msg} \\
c_{msg} + 1 & \text{if } l' = l_{msg} \text{ and } l' > l \\
0 & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}$$

### 2.3 Verdict for Sub-Millisecond OME
For a sub-millisecond execution target, **HLC** provides a lower baseline latency profile because it avoids the mandatory $2\epsilon$ commit-wait penalty. However, to guarantee strict serializability without anomalies across regions, HLC must be coupled with a **Multi-Region Paxos consensus layer** that forces a synchronization barrier on cross-shard interactions.

